<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iolo Morganwg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1747">1747</a>–<a href="/years/1826">1826</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Edward Williams</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Williams, better known by his bardic name Iolo Morganwg ('Iolo of Glamorgan'), was a Welsh antiquarian, poet and collector, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a>. He was seen as an expert collector of medieval Welsh literature, but after his death it emerged that he had forged several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them poems he attributed to Dafydd ap Gwilym in 1789 and the third series of Welsh Triads in The Myvyrian Archaiology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> (1801–1807). In 1792 he founded the Gorsedd, a community of Welsh bards, at Primrose Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, through which he co-opted the Eisteddfod revival. The philosophy of his forgeries has had an enormous impact on neo-Druidism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iolo-morganwg.jpg" alt="Iolo Morganwg" width="330" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Edward_Williams,_bardd_braint_a_defod_(4672175).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Williams was born at Pen-onn, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vale-of-glamorgan">Vale of Glamorgan</a>, and raised in the village of Flemingston. He followed his father as a stonemason. In Glamorgan he took an interest in collecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and learnt to compose Welsh poetry from poets such as Lewis Hopkin, Rhys Morgan and especially Siôn Bradford. In 1773 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where the antiquary Owen Jones introduced him to the city's Welsh literary community and he joined the Gwyneddigion Society. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1777, married and tried farming without success, and in these years he produced his first forgeries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Williams produced many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> as evidence that ancient Druidic tradition had survived the Roman conquest, the conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the persecution of bards under Edward I. His forgeries develop an elaborate mystical philosophy that he claimed continued ancient Druidic practice. It fused Christian and Arthurian influences with a romanticism comparable to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-blake">William Blake</a> and James Macpherson, and it set out concentric rings of existence from Annwn through Abred and Ceugant to Gwynfyd. Part of his aim was to assert the Welshness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> against the idea that North Wales kept the purest Welsh traditions.</p><p>He helped Owen Jones and William Owen Pughe prepare The Myvyrian Archaiology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, which included a false Brut chronicle and a book attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cadoc">Saint Cadoc</a>, and he altered authentic triads. His collection of Poems, Lyric and Pastoral, published in 1794, is essentially his only genuine work. The Gorsedd was founded at a ceremony on Primrose Hill on 21 June 1792 that he organised according to what he claimed were ancient Druidic rites. He also devised the Bardic Alphabet, Coelbren y Beirdd, and became a Unitarian by 1799. Barddas, published in 1862 and 1874, presented more of his forgeries as a translation of works by Llywelyn Siôn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Towards the end of the 19th century the grammarian Sir John Morris-Jones helped to expose him as a forger, and Morris-Jones said it would be an age before Welsh history and literature were clean of the traces of his dirty fingers. After the First World War Griffith John Williams made the first full study of his work from papers given to the National Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1917, and he established that the poems attributed to Dafydd ap Gwilym were forgeries. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taliesin">Taliesin</a> collected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in 26 volumes, a selection of which appeared as the Iolo Manuscripts in 1848.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druid">Druid</a>'s Prayer is still used by the Gorsedd and by neo-Druid groups. Some of his altered texts of medieval Welsh works are better known than the originals, even into the 21st century. A school in Cowbridge, Ysgol Iolo Morganwg, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iolo Morganwg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Wales the bardic order, revived and codified by the poet and forger <b>Iolo Morganwg</b>, lives on in the eisteddfods.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>James Macpherson’s poems of Ossian, published in the 1760s as translations of ancient Gaelic verse but largely his own, were read across Europe; <b>Iolo Morganwg</b> devised the bardic Gorsedd in 1792; and national movements in Ireland, Scotland, Wales and Brittany looked to the Celts as ancestors.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llancarfan">Llancarfan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Flemingston, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-3.37&amp;year=1798" aria-label="Llancarfan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4474 383 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Iolo Morganwg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.42,-3.37&amp;year=1798">What was here in 1798? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
